Terraform is an open-source infrastructure-as-code tool developed by HashiCorp that lets you manage infrastructure by describing what you want, not by writing step-by-step commands. In Terraform, you write configuration files in HashiCorp Configuration Language, commonly called HCL, and Terraform translates those declarations into the actions needed to reach the desired state.

Terraform relies on providers to interact with cloud, SaaS, and infrastructure APIs. A provider supplies the integration details, including how Terraform authenticates and how it understands the API’s schema, so your configuration can request real infrastructure changes without you manually calling APIs. Within a configuration, a resource represents a manageable object such as a network, an instance, or a bucket, and Terraform tracks that resource as something it can create, update, or delete.

In contrast, a data source reads existing information without necessarily creating a managed resource. This distinction matters because resources are part of Terraform’s managed state, while data sources are used to look up values that other parts of the configuration can reference. When you reference one resource from another, Terraform builds a dependency graph, which determines operation ordering so that prerequisites are handled before dependents.

Terraform also supports reusable configuration patterns using variables, outputs, locals, expressions, and functions. Variables let you parameterize values without changing the overall structure, while outputs expose selected values for users or other automation. Locals and expressions help compute or transform values, and functions provide reusable logic, which keeps configurations declarative rather than turning them into procedural scripts.

Here is a minimal example that creates one concrete AWS S3 bucket and outputs its name.

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "example" {
  bucket = "my-unique-bucket-name-12345"
}

output "bucket_name" {
  value = aws_s3_bucket.example.bucket
}
```
